WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.790
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.100
What is super resolution?

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.990
Super resolution is process where our input
is a coarse, low resolution image, and the

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.779
output is the same image, but now with more
details and in high resolution.

00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:21.380
We'll also refer to this process as image
upscaling.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.699
And in this piece of work, we are interested
in performing single image super resolution,

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:32.200
which means that no additional data is presented
to the algorithm that could help the process.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.440
Despite the incredible results seen in practically
any of the crime solving television shows

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.960
out there, our intuition would perhaps say
that this problem, for the first sight, sounds

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.140
impossible.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:49.660
How could one mathematically fill in the details
when these details are completely unknown?

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.450
Well, that's only kind of true.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.790
Let's not confuse super resolution with image
inpainting, where we essentially cut an entire

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:03.039
part out of an image and try to replace it
leaning on our knowledge of the surroundings

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:04.670
of the missing part.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.020
That's a different problem.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:11.400
Here, the entirety of the image is known,
and the details require some enhancing.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.490
This particular method is not based on neural
networks, but is still a learning-based technique.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:22.560
The cool thing here, is that we can use a
training dataset, that is, for all intents

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.020
and purposes, arbitrarily large.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:30.420
We can just grab a high resolution image,
convert it to a lower resolution and we immediately

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.170
have our hands on a training example for the
learning algorithm.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.590
These would be the before and after images,
if you will.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:43.979
And here, during learning, the image is subdivided
into small image patches, and buckets are

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:49.719
created to aggregate the information between
patches that share similar features.

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:54.640
These features include brightness, textures,
and the orientation of the edges.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.899
The technique looks at how the small and large
resolution images relate to each other when

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:02.380
viewed through the lens of these features.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.280
Two remarkably interesting things arose from
this experiment:

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.869
- one, it outperforms existing neural network-based
techniques,

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:17.510
- two, it only uses 10 thousand images, and
one hour of training time, which is in the

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.580
world of deep neural networks, is so little,
it's completely unheard of.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.580
Insanity.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.650
Really, really well done.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:30.020
Some tricks are involved to keep the memory
consumption low, the paper discusses how it

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.630
is done, and there are also plenty of other
details within, make sure to have a look,

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.190
as always, it is linked in the video description.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:42.930
It can either be run directly on the low resolution
image, or alternatively we can first run a

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:48.720
cheap and naive decade-old upscaling algorithm,
and run this technique on this upscaled output

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.010
to improve it.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:55.040
Note that super resolution is a remarkably
competitive field of research, there are hundreds

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.110
and hundreds of papers appearing on this every
year, and almost every single one of them

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.560
seems to be miles ahead of the previous ones.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.590
Where in reality, the truth is that most of
these methods have different weaknesses and

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:14.010
strengths, and so far I haven't seen any technique
that would be viable for universal use.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:18.800
To make sure that a large number of cases
is covered, the authors posted a sizeable

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.420
supplementary document with comparisons.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.810
This gives so much more credence to the results.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:29.560
I am hoping to see a more widespread adoption
of this in future papers in this area.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.680
For now, when viewing websites, I feel that
we are close to the point where we could choose

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.370
to transmit only the lower resolution images
through the network and perform super resolution

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.460
on them locally on our phones and computers.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.820
This will lead to significant savings on network
bandwidth.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.250
We are living amazing times indeed.

